  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 557 PM MDT Mon Aug 24 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nUpper-level ridging will help keep most of the region dry through \nWednesday. The main exception being Southwest MT, where a slow \nreturn of monsoonal moisture with mini vorticity maximums will \nallow for isolated showers and thunderstorms to develop. Today, \nprecipitation is more focused towards the MT/ID border, and \ntomorrow/Wednesday is focused along and south of I-90. Although \nthere will be enough moisture for convection through Wednesday, a \nvery dry surface layer will bring isolated dry thunderstorms. Deep\ninverted V sounding profiles will also bring an isolated strong \nwind threat to convection (low confidence in severe wind gusts). \nCoverage in showers and thunderstorms increase northwards \nthroughout the week, becoming widespread across the region on \nThursday. A trough coming onshore Thursday through the weekend \nwill increase winds aloft and keep daily precipitation chances \nacross the region. Better forcing and shear Thursday signals a \npotential threat for isolated strong to severe wind gusts, mainly \nin the mountain valleys/foothills of the Continental Divide. \nTemperatures gradually warm throughout the week with increasing \nsouthwest flow aloft. \n\nDuring the weekend, the trough axis moves across the region. This \nbrings a better synoptic setup for widespread precipitation on \nSunday. How much precipitation and where the better corridor of \nprecipitation set up depends on the evolution of this upper-level\nwave (a compact closed low vs a broader wave/trough). This trough\nwill bring cooler temperatures to the region for next week along \nwith widespread breezy winds for the weekend (with uncertainty \nhow windy it gets based on the trough evolution). -Wilson",
